  "family_or_category": "business",
  "caption": "For twenty years, hard skills like coding and data analysis paid the most. That is starting to flip\n\nMoravec's paradox explains why: what is hard for people is often easy for machines, and the reverse. AI now writes code, but reading a room, earning trust and negotiating a deal are still human skills, and in business they close sales and keep clients\n\nSave this for your next career decision. More at edlintics.com\n\n#edlintics #businessstudents #careers #business",
  "caption_x": "Hard skills are getting easier for machines, and soft skills are worth more. Moravec's paradox in 35 seconds. edlintics.com #edlintics",
  "first_comment": "Which skill do you think machines will master last: negotiating, managing a team or selling? Reply with one. More short business explainers at edlintics.com",
  "maths_check": "No calculation in this reel. Fact check: Hans Moravec and others described the paradox in the 1980s (Moravec, Mind Children, 1988). Computers handle formal reasoning such as logic tests and chess, while perception and movement that a one-year-old manages are hard for machines.",
